┗┻┛┗┻┛┗━╸╹ ╹┗━╸  ╹┗━┛   ┗━┛┗━╸┗┛

multiple-choice questions Qwen3.8-27B


a json grammar makes the model write a valid answer.
this skips the writing and reads the odds of each answer instead.

    llm.decide(state, "did the deployment succeed?", ["yes", "no", "unclear"])
    -> {"yes": 0.91, "no": 0.03, "unclear": 0.06}


quick demo:

    $ ./run.sh uv run example.py


the demo then asks 21 yes/no questions about one 8k-char incident report.
decide_many pays for that state once and reads all 21 answers off the shared
prefix cache, the decide loop pays for it 21 times. measured on a 24 GB 3090 Ti
with the weights loaded 4-bit (bf16 needs 52 GB, TODO redo on one h200):

    cache path      b, per-suffix replay of the prefilled hybrid cache (a ran out of memory)
    decide_many     6.8 s
    decide loop     33.4 s
    disagreements   0 of 21 argmax, max |dp| 0.0005


four ways to answer the same 21 questions, same 4-bit weights on the 3090 Ti.
jev reads the letter logits, json writes {"answer": "A"} under a schema grammar;
think means the model generates a reasoning trace first and the answer is read
after it. all four agreed with the hand written key and with each other:

    condition    correct   sec/q    out tok/q    p(key)
    jev          21/21       1.65         0.0       0.998
    jev+think    21/21      13.50       106.3       1.000
    json         21/21       2.49         7.0           -
    json+think   21/21      16.53       136.1           -

    $ ./run.sh uv run bench.py


References:

- OpenJev, the same idea on a 4B model: https://github.com/TheoLeeCJ/openjev
- Jev, the closed service it reproduces: https://typesafe.ai
- Qwen3.8-27B: https://huggingface.co/Qwen/Qwen3.8-27B
